 ## Filling it in
 
-- **Exact means exact.** Absolute paths, package names with their scope, full versions, the full commit message (with any trailer lines the session requires), and the exact text of any file edit. For edits longer than a line or two, write a patch or message file first and pass its path, which also avoids shell-quoting mistakes.
-- **Approval first.** Every outward-facing step must already be approved by the user in this session before it goes into a brief.
-- **Every step starts with `cd`, and every path is quoted.** Each of the agent's tool calls starts fresh: the working directory and shell variables don't carry over. So start every command with `cd "<absolute path>" &&`, and pass fixed paths as inputs instead of `$(mktemp -d)` results. Quote paths even when they have no spaces, because the agent copies the style it sees, and Windows paths often do have spaces.
-- **Name the shell, and write for it.** On Windows the Bash tool is Git Bash (`/c/Users/...` paths). Keep command arguments ASCII: accented characters can get mangled between Git Bash and `node.exe`, so use `è`-style escapes inside JS strings.
-- **Create local commits and tags yourself, then pin them.** A commit or tag that records your decisions is made on the main model before launch (see SKILL.md). The brief then gives the commit SHA and, for an annotated tag, the tag object SHA (`git rev-parse <tag>`), and the first steps check HEAD and the tag against both. That way nothing lands on a commit nobody looked at, and a tag with the same name made by someone else can't pass. Take tag names and messages from the repo's existing tags: `git for-each-ref refs/tags --format='%(refname:short) %(objecttype) %(contents:subject)'`.
-- **Read the remote with `git ls-remote`.** Get the remote's current SHAs from `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main refs/tags/<tag>`, not from `origin/main`: a fresh or copied clone may have no remote-tracking ref, and one that exists can be stale.
-- **Make steps safe to rerun.** Check whether the thing already exists before creating it, and have the agent report `skipped` with evidence if it does. Put these checks before the irreversible step (check the tag is free on the remote and the version is free on npm before publishing or pushing the tag, not after). A corrected brief after a partial failure is then safe to run.
-- **Pin the tarball when build output is gitignored.** If `dist/` (or whatever `files` ships) is in `.gitignore`, `git status` says nothing about what will be published. Record the `integrity` and `entryCount` from `npm pack --dry-run --json --ignore-scripts` when you review the file list, and add a step just before the publish that checks both values. Tell the agent to match the `"integrity": "…"` and `"entryCount": N` lines, because the JSON's outer shape differs between npm versions. `--ignore-scripts` stops a `prepack` script from rebuilding in between. If either value differs, the agent stops; it doesn't rebuild.
-- **Keep network commands non-interactive.** On Windows, Git Credential Manager opens a login window instead of printing a prompt, so a background push hangs until the tool times out. Prefix git network commands (`push`, `pull`, `fetch`, `ls-remote`) with `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GCM_INTERACTIVE=never` so they fail loudly. Run `npm publish` with `</dev/null`. Pass `--yes`/`-y` where the tool has one and the action is approved, and avoid commands that open an editor (`git commit` without `-m`/`-F`, `git tag -a` without `-m`).
